Provider marks (#2542). The application shows the mark of an AI provider next to its agent: in the usage rows, in the
launcher and in the Library's agent cards. Each mark belongs to its owner and is not licensed by any licence above. It
is drawn only to name the owner's product, as the provider publishes it (the provider's own file, unaltered, in the
provider's own light and dark variants), and never implies that the provider endorses, sponsors or is affiliated with
MoltenTerm or DiamondForge. The set is frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/provider-marks.ts; a provider that
withdraws its permission has its mark removed from it. Where each mark comes from, and its terms:
- Anthropic, the Claude mark (Claude Code). Wave Terminal's file, shipped as it came:
  frontend/app/asset/claude-color.svg
  Terms: https://www.anthropic.com/legal/trademark-guidelines (the guidelines keep the use of the marks to what
  Anthropic permits; this use is the one Wave Terminal made, and is open to Anthropic's review).
- OpenAI, the Blossom (Codex), from the logos kit https://cdn.openai.com/brand/openai-logos.zip of
  https://openai.com/brand/ ("Use the logo only when it directly relates to OpenAI services", no colour added, not
  featured more prominently than MoltenTerm's own marks). The kit's files, shipped as they came:
  OpenAI-logos/SVGs/OAI_OpenAI-Blossom_White.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/openai-blossom-white.svg
  OpenAI-logos/SVGs/OAI_OpenAI-Blossom_Black.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/openai-blossom-black.svg
  Terms: https://openai.com/brand/#usage-terms
- Anomaly, the OpenCode logo, from https://opencode.ai/brand/files/ ("plugins, providers and tools that work with
  OpenCode can say so", no recolouring, drawn at 16 x 20 px or more). The files, shipped as they came:
  opencode-logo-transparent-dark.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/opencode-logo-dark.svg
  opencode-logo-transparent-light.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/opencode-logo-light.svg
  Terms: https://opencode.ai/brand
- Anysphere, the Cursor cube (Cursor CLI), from the asset bundle of https://cursor.com/brand
  (https://ptht05hbb1ssoooe.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/assets/brand/cursor-brand-assets.zip). The page states no
  rule for third parties beyond the name ("Cursor", never "Cursor AI" or "Cursor Code"). The files, shipped as they
  came:
  General Logos/Cube/SVG/CUBE_2D_DARK.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/cursor-cube-2d-dark.svg
  General Logos/Cube/SVG/CUBE_2D_LIGHT.svg
  frontend/moltenterm-shell/provider-marks/assets/cursor-cube-2d-light.svg
  Terms: https://cursor.com/brand
The agents of other providers keep the AI icon, each for a reason recorded in provider-marks.ts:
- Moonshot AI (Kimi Code): https://www.kimi.com/policies/logo-usage-terms licenses its logo "solely for editorial,
  media, and non-commercial promotional uses" and forbids recolouring it, which a row of an application is not.
- Google (Gemini CLI, Antigravity CLI), GitHub (Copilot CLI) and Alibaba Cloud (Qwen Code) publish no mark with
  guidelines for naming their product in third-party software, or none that was found.
- xAI (Grok Build) and Mistral AI (Mistral Vibe): their brand pages answered HTTP 403 to a program, so no file was
  taken.

